WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:07.760
So now we come to the part where we ask the question, why do we need those parts?

00:07.760 --> 00:11.480
Why do we need to extract the date parts from a date?

00:11.480 --> 00:13.440
So let's have the following use cases.

00:13.440 --> 00:19.040
The first use case of extracting the part is doing data aggregations and reporting.

00:19.040 --> 00:23.320
Sometimes you are building like reports based on our data, and sometimes you have to aggregate our

00:23.320 --> 00:25.640
data by specific time unit.

00:25.680 --> 00:30.200
Like for example, we are building a reports in order to show the sales by year.

00:30.200 --> 00:34.440
So we have different years and we are aggregating the data based on the year.

00:34.440 --> 00:39.240
Or you want to drill down to more details where you want to aggregate the data by that quarter.

00:39.240 --> 00:44.080
So in this report we are showing the sales by quarter Q1 234.

00:44.120 --> 00:49.640
Or you decide to go in more details where you show the report says sales by month, and then you start

00:49.680 --> 00:51.760
aggregating your data by the month.

00:51.760 --> 00:54.080
So you have January, February, March and so on.

00:54.080 --> 00:59.400
So as you can see, we can use those different parts in order to aggregate the data based on it.

00:59.400 --> 01:04.110
And these different parts can offer us different analyses with different details.

01:04.110 --> 01:05.430
So now we have the following task.

01:05.430 --> 01:08.910
And it says how many orders were placed each year.

01:08.910 --> 01:11.670
So that means we have to group up our data by the year.

01:11.670 --> 01:13.510
And we have to count the number of orders.

01:13.550 --> 01:14.830
Let's go and solve it.

01:14.830 --> 01:16.510
So let's go with the select.

01:16.510 --> 01:17.310
And now what do we need.

01:17.350 --> 01:19.830
We need the order date.

01:19.830 --> 01:22.350
This is going to indicate when the order is placed.

01:22.350 --> 01:25.750
So and we have to go and count the star.

01:25.790 --> 01:28.390
So this is going to be number of orders.

01:28.750 --> 01:32.790
And from our table sales orders.

01:32.790 --> 01:35.830
And we have to group up by the order dates.

01:36.110 --> 01:37.110
So that's it.

01:37.110 --> 01:38.270
Let's go and execute it.

01:38.270 --> 01:43.470
So now in the output we are getting the number of orders but by the order date.

01:43.470 --> 01:44.750
So we are still not there.

01:44.750 --> 01:46.830
We have to have it as a year.

01:46.830 --> 01:49.070
So we don't need the whole date information.

01:49.070 --> 01:51.470
We need only the year information.

01:51.470 --> 01:55.830
So that means we have to go and extract the part year in order to do that.

01:55.830 --> 01:57.030
We can do it like this.

01:57.030 --> 02:01.980
So we can go with the year and we have it as well in the group by.

02:02.020 --> 02:02.900
So that's it.

02:02.900 --> 02:04.300
Let's go and execute it.

02:04.300 --> 02:07.500
And with that as you can see, we got the number of orders for each year.

02:07.500 --> 02:12.260
And since in our data we have only 2025, we will get only one row so that the task is solved.

02:12.260 --> 02:15.660
We are now aggregating the data on the level of the year.

02:15.700 --> 02:19.100
Now let's have another task which is the same but only different parts.

02:19.100 --> 02:21.580
How many orders were placed each month?

02:21.700 --> 02:24.300
So we have to go and change it to a month.

02:24.860 --> 02:25.740
It's very simple.

02:25.740 --> 02:29.500
We're going to use the function month and as well in the groupby.

02:29.540 --> 02:31.540
So let's go and execute it.

02:31.580 --> 02:33.820
And now as you can see in the output we don't have one row.

02:33.820 --> 02:34.860
Now we have three rows.

02:34.900 --> 02:37.700
And that's because we have three months inside our data.

02:37.700 --> 02:40.940
And for each month we will get the total number of orders.

02:40.940 --> 02:46.260
So for the January we have for February we have four and March we have two orders.

02:46.300 --> 02:49.100
Now you might say, you know what, I don't want the months as numbers.

02:49.100 --> 02:51.260
I would like to have the full name of the month.

02:51.260 --> 02:54.860
So in order to do that we're going to go and use the function date name.

02:54.860 --> 02:56.900
So let's go and use date name.

02:57.380 --> 02:59.500
And then we have to specify the date part.

02:59.500 --> 03:04.680
It's going to be the month and the value going to be the order date, and we have to have the same thing

03:04.720 --> 03:06.120
as well in the group by.

03:06.240 --> 03:08.040
So let's go and execute it.

03:08.080 --> 03:12.800
Now you can see in the output we are getting the full name of the month which is easier to read.

03:12.880 --> 03:18.640
So this is one of the use cases why we need to extract parts from a date in order to aggregate the data

03:18.640 --> 03:20.000
on a specific level.

03:23.920 --> 03:25.200
So now let's have the following task.

03:25.240 --> 03:30.360
And it says show all orders that were placed during the month of February.

03:30.360 --> 03:31.880
So that means we don't need all the orders.

03:31.880 --> 03:35.720
We need only a subset of the orders based on the order dates.

03:35.760 --> 03:37.000
Now let's go and check the data.

03:37.000 --> 03:43.720
So select star first from sales orders and let's go and execute it.

03:43.720 --> 03:45.400
So now with that we have our ten orders.

03:45.440 --> 03:50.800
Now if you check the order date over here you can see that we have orders in January, February and

03:50.800 --> 03:51.280
March.

03:51.320 --> 03:55.240
Now we are interested only on the orders that were placed in February.

03:55.240 --> 03:56.560
So only these subsets.

03:56.560 --> 04:00.680
So that means we have now to filter the data based on the month information.

04:00.840 --> 04:05.270
So what we're going to do we're going to have a Where clause and now we don't need the whole order date.

04:05.270 --> 04:07.150
We need only the part month.

04:07.310 --> 04:11.430
So we're going to go with the month and order date.

04:11.630 --> 04:16.310
And this is going to be equal to two since the output is going to be in number.

04:16.310 --> 04:18.270
So let's go and execute it.

04:18.310 --> 04:18.470
Now.

04:18.470 --> 04:20.510
As you can see SQL did filter the data.

04:20.510 --> 04:25.390
And in the output we have only the orders were placed in the month of February.

04:25.390 --> 04:27.670
So this is as well a very common use case.

04:27.670 --> 04:29.350
Why do we need the parts.

04:29.350 --> 04:34.830
We use it in order to filter the data based on specific part of the dates.

04:34.830 --> 04:36.950
So as you can see, it's very quick and easy.

04:36.990 --> 04:41.270
And here my recommendation is that if you are filtering the data, always use the numbers.

04:41.270 --> 04:47.190
So always use a date function that gives you a number because it's always faster to search for integers

04:47.190 --> 04:50.790
instead of searching for a character or for string.

04:50.790 --> 04:54.790
So don't use the date name function in order to search or filter for the data.

04:54.790 --> 04:59.950
It's better to use the date, part or month, year, and day since you can work with numbers and numbers

04:59.950 --> 05:03.670
are always faster to retrieve data and to filter your informations.
